+  {
+   "id": "analysis",
+   "heading": {
+    "zh": "为什么会这样",
+    "en": "Why It Worked"
+   },
+   "paras": [
+    {
+     "zh": "这次调查有说服力，靠的不是拆把手，而是问法：不只问死者喝了什么，也看没喝的人怎么样。济贫院和啤酒厂离井很近，却几乎没人受害；E太太和侄女远离宽街，却喝了那口井的水。一种解释是（作者推断）：这些“例外”比几十个典型病例更难用别的说法解释，比如“这一带空气不好”。",
+     "en": "What made the inquiry persuasive was not the handle but the questions: not only what the dead had drunk, but what happened to those who had not. The workhouse and the brewery stood next to the well and were barely touched; Mrs E and her niece lived far away but drank its water. One interpretation, the author's, is that such exceptions are harder to explain any other way, for example by bad air in the district, than dozens of typical cases.",
+     "kind": "interpretation",
+     "ev": [
+      "B-E4",
+      "B-E5",
+      "B-E9"
+     ]
+    },
+    {
+     "zh": "怀特黑德的转变同样要紧。他本以为调查会推翻水泵说，结果找到了最早的病例和污物进井的路径。一个抱着反对意见去查的人，查完改变了看法，这样的结论往往比一群本来就同意的人互相印证更可信。",
+     "en": "Whitehead's change of mind matters just as much. He had expected the inquiry to disprove the pump theory, and instead found the earliest case and a route by which waste reached the well. A conclusion reached by someone who went looking to refute it is often more convincing than agreement among people who were convinced from the start.",
+     "kind": "interpretation",
+     "ev": [
+      "B-E10",
+      "B-E12",
+      "B-E13"
+     ]
+    },
+    {
+     "zh": "证据也有边界。斯诺的饮水情况多是从别人那里打听来的，有问不到的，也可能有记错的。拆把手时疫情已在消退，所以不能说是拆把手终止了疫情；一种可能是，它挡住的是之后继续来喝这口井的人。",
+     "en": "The evidence has limits. Most of what Snow knew about drinking habits came second-hand; some cases could not be traced and some answers may have been wrong. The outbreak was already fading when the handle came off, so it cannot be said that removing it ended the epidemic. One possibility is that it kept later visitors from drinking at the well.",
+     "kind": "uncertainty",
+     "ev": [
+      "B-E2",
+      "B-E6",
+      "B-E8"
+     ]
+    }
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "id": "today",
+   "heading": {
+    "zh": "今天可以怎么用",
+    "en": "How You Can Use It Today"
+   },
+   "paras": [
+    {
+     "zh": "假设你在一家做配送调度的小公司，连续三天傍晚订单大面积超时，人人都怀疑前天夜里那次发布。先别急着回滚：学斯诺先问一句，谁没事——那两个没超时的片区，是不是没走同一套调度服务？把没出事的人群列成清单，常常比盯着出事的那批人更快找到边界。",
+     "en": "Suppose you work for a small delivery-scheduling company, and for three evenings in a row a large share of orders has run late, and everyone suspects the release from the night before last. Don't rush to roll it back: do as Snow did and ask first who was fine — did the two districts with no delays run a different scheduling service? Drawing up a list of the groups that were untouched often finds the boundary faster than staring at the group that broke.",
+     "kind": "modern",
+     "ev": []
+    },
+    {
+     "zh": "复盘会上，一位原本想证明不是新流程之过的同事翻出更早的日志，发现了真正的触发点。把他的查证过程原样记下来，哪怕结论不讨喜；同时把结论分成已确认和机制推测两栏，别让一份整齐的叙述掩盖证据到底有多硬。",
+     "en": "In the review meeting, a colleague who originally wanted to prove it was not the new process dug out earlier logs and found the real trigger. Write up his verification process exactly as it happened, even if the conclusion is unwelcome; and at the same time split the findings into two columns, confirmed and mechanism conjecture, so that a tidy narrative doesn't hide how solid the evidence really is.",
+     "kind": "modern",
+     "ev": []
+    }
+   ]
