+ "reflection": {
+  "type": "如何纠错",
+  "scenario": {
+   "zh": "虚构情境：一家连锁烘焙坊的三家门店在同一个周末出现顾客腹泻投诉，公司初步怀疑一批奶油；A门店已停用该批次，之后仍有零星投诉。负责采购的质量经理最初想证明不是奶油的问题。",
+   "en": "Fictional situation: three stores of a bakery chain receive customer complaints of diarrhoea over the same weekend, and the company first suspects a batch of cream; store A has already stopped using that batch, yet sporadic complaints continue. The quality manager in charge of purchasing first wants to prove that the cream is not at fault."
+  },
+  "question": {
+   "zh": "在化验结果出来之前，你会依据什么证据停用这批奶油、通知顾客，还是保持沉默？停用的代价是销毁库存并承认风险，不停用的代价是可能继续有人受害——哪一种错误你觉得更难挽回？",
+   "en": "Before the lab results come back, on what evidence would you stop using this batch of cream and notify customers, or stay silent? The cost of stopping is destroying stock and admitting a risk; the cost of not stopping is that more people may be harmed — which mistake do you think is harder to undo?"
+  },
+  "example": {
+   "assumptions": {
+    "zh": "假设投诉例数尚少、因果未确认，但该批次奶油确实覆盖三家门店；假设公开通知会触发监管上报流程。",
+    "en": "Assume the number of cases is still small and causation is unconfirmed, but that batch of cream really does cover all three stores; assume that a public notice would trigger a regulatory reporting process."
+   },
+   "steps": {
+    "zh": "先列谁没事：未使用该批次的门店或产品线有无同类投诉；核对发病时间是否晚于批次到货时间；请一位不想要既定结论的同事独立复核订单与制作记录；设定临时下架的触发线，例如出现第二例与该批次相关的病例即停用。",
+    "en": "First list who was fine: are there similar complaints from stores or product lines that did not use this batch; check whether the onset times come after the batch arrived; ask a colleague who does not want the settled conclusion to review the orders and production records independently; set a trigger line for temporary withdrawal, for example stopping the batch as soon as a second case linked to it appears."
+   },
+   "alternative": {
+    "zh": "另一种合理选择是先只在A门店小范围停用并留样化验、暂不公开通知：理由是证据尚弱，避免错误归因和恐慌；代价是若判断错误会延误。",
+    "en": "Another reasonable choice is to withdraw the batch only at store A on a small scale, keep samples for testing, and not make a public notice for now: the reason is that the evidence is still weak and this avoids wrong attribution and panic; the cost is that a wrong call delays action."
+   }
+  }
+ },
+ "uncertainty_note": {
+  "zh": "主要事实与数字出自斯诺1855年的著作及他提交调查委员会的报告；怀特黑德的转变、40号女婴和化粪池调查出自同一份委员会报告里他本人的记述与委员会的调查。两者是不同的来源基础，但并非完全独立：斯诺本人也是委员会成员。饮水情况多为转述，有问不到的病例；E太太与侄女两例来自她儿子的转述。拆把手与疫情下降的因果无法确定。1854年没有细菌学说，“霍乱毒物”是斯诺当时的说法。本篇未取得中央卫生机构对此事的意见，因此不写其立场。",
+  "en": "Most facts and figures come from Snow's 1855 book and his report to the inquiry committee. Whitehead's change of mind, the infant at No. 40 and the cesspool inquiry come from his own account and the committee's inquiry in the same 1855 report. These are different source bases but not fully independent: Snow himself sat on the committee. Drinking habits were mostly reported second-hand, and some cases could not be traced; the cases of Mrs E and her niece come from her son. Whether removing the handle caused the decline cannot be established. Germ theory did not exist in 1854; \"cholera poison\" is Snow's own term. This article has not drawn on the views of national health authorities, so it does not describe their position."
